+
+# Implement JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386) applyMergePatch
+
+Some of our clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format. Implement JSON Merge
+Patch, as specified in RFC 7386. It is independent of JSON Patch (task-002): it needs no operation list
+and no JSON Pointer.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Export a function `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies the merge patch to
+  the target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+- Follow RFC 7386 section 2 exactly:
+  - When `patch` is an object, the target is merged member by member: a member whose patch value is
+    `null` is removed from the target (when present); any other member is set to the result of merging
+    the target's member (or `{}` when the target has none) with the patch value, recursively.
+  - When the target is not an object (a scalar, `null` or an array), it is replaced by `{}` before its
+    members are merged.
+  - When `patch` is not an object (a scalar, `null` or an array), it replaces the target entirely.
+  - Arrays are never merged: an array in the patch replaces the target value as a whole, and `null`
+    elements inside it are kept, since only object members are processed.
+- The inputs are not mutated: the result is a new value, and it shares no object with the patch.
+- Throw an error when `target` or `patch` is not a JSON value, as `applyPatch` does for its document.
+- No runtime dependency is added; `src/index.ts` keeps its existing exports and public types.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- Tests are written first and fail for the missing behaviour before the implementation (red-first, since
+  the behaviour is new).
+- Tests cover the RFC 7386 section 3 examples (each row of its table), and the cases above: nested
+  removal, a `null` member with no target member, a non-object target with an object patch, arrays
+  replaced whole, a non-object patch replacing an object, a patch with `null` as the whole document,
+  non-mutation of both inputs, and rejection of a non-JSON target or patch.
+- Every behaviour has at least one happy-path test and one edge- or error-path test.
+- `npm test` passes, with the existing tests still passing.
+
+## Notes
+
+- Reuse `isJsonValue` and `setMember` from `src/index.ts`.
+- `"__proto__"` is a plain member name here too, as in task-002.
+
+## Outcome
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` is exported from `src/index.ts`, after `applyPatch`. It follows RFC 7386
+section 2 through a private recursive `merge`: a `null` object member removes the target member, an object
+member merges recursively into the target member (or `{}`), a non-object target is replaced by `{}` before
+an object patch is merged, arrays and non-object patches replace the target whole.
+
+The target is copied with `structuredClone`, so neither input is mutated and the result shares no object
+with the patch. `target` and `patch` are checked with `isJsonValue`, as `applyPatch` checks its document.
+
+Tests: the section 3 examples of RFC 7386, the cases listed in the requirements, and a `__proto__` member.
+They were written first and failed with the export missing. `npm test` runs 59 tests, all passing.
+
+No TypeScript compiler is installed in this environment, so the strict type check of `tsconfig.json` was
+not run; the tests run the TypeScript source through Node's type stripping.
